# goal acp2api

Expose each coding CLI as one OpenAI model id, so a router can build failover
chains out of subscriptions instead of API keys.

The constraint that shapes everything here: **the subscription must be spent by the
CLI itself**. The CLI is spawned and speaks ACP over stdio; no OAuth token is read,
replayed, or forwarded. Every project surveyed on 2026-08-09 that handled quota
exhaustion well did it by borrowing the vendor's token and calling the vendor's real
HTTP API — engineering quality was inversely correlated with legality. This is the
other branch of that fork, and its known cost is that a bad status code is the only
signal a caller ever gets.

# lang Node 22+, ESM, no build step

JavaScript, not TypeScript: the ACP ecosystem is npm-first, and a build step buys
nothing at this size. Tests are `node --test`; there is no test framework.

# dep Four packages, and a reason for each

Required: `@agentclientprotocol/sdk` (the protocol, zero transitive deps) and `yaml`
(config). Optional: the adapters `@agentclientprotocol/claude-agent-acp` and
`@agentclientprotocol/codex-acp` — only `general` agents work without them, and
`@openai/codex` is a large download nobody using `general` should pay for.

They are **dependencies, not global installs**, so their versions cannot drift with
whatever `claude`/`codex` happens to be on PATH. `claude-agent-acp` bundles
`@anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk` and reads the login in `~/.claude` — that directory
must exist (and be mounted, in a container) for it to work at all.

Do not add an HTTP framework. `node:http` covers the HTTP surface, and every
dependency here is one more thing that can break an unattended server.

# bad Never spawn a preset by its bare bin name {#global_install}

A dependency's bin is linked into **this package's** `node_modules/.bin`, which is
not on PATH when the package is installed globally. Spawning `claude-agent-acp`
therefore works from a checkout and fails for every `npm i -g acp2api` user — the
worst kind of bug, invisible to the author.

`resolvePreset()` resolves the adapter's `package.json`, reads its `bin` entry and
spawns `process.execPath <entry>`: no PATH lookup, no shebang, no exec bit. It falls
back to the bare name when the adapter is absent, because it is optional.

# build make

`make test` (offline, no subscription spent), `make check` (validates the shipped
example config), `make spec` (verifies companion HINT contracts), `make verify`
(`npm ci` + test + check + spec + pack — the pre-publish gate).

Local publishing requires the operator's `.npmrc` and interactive web approval;
`OTP=123456` remains available for an account using TOTP. The release workflow uses
npm trusted publishing through OIDC instead of a stored registry token.

`verify` uses `npm ci`, not `npm install`: a suite that passes only against a drifted
tree proves nothing about what a consumer installs.

# rule Split OpenAI parameters by consequence, not by support {#param_policy}

`session/prompt` carries exactly `{sessionId, prompt, _meta}` — no sampling knobs,
no tools, no response format. There is no lower layer that exposes them either:
`claude-agent-acp` has process-level model, thinking-budget and settings controls,
but no temperature to set at any layer. This is a property of the product, not of
the bridge; do not go looking for a flag that exposes it.

The useful question is what breaks if the request proceeds, and the two answers
differ enough to need different handling:

- **Style** — `temperature`, `top_p`, `seed`, penalties. Every client library sends
  them unasked, so a 400 would reject nearly every real request over a difference
  the caller cannot perceive. **Accept and ignore.**
- **Contract** — `response_format`, `n > 1`, `audio`. Ignoring these returns
  something that means the wrong thing, and nothing gives the caller its guarantee
  back. **400, always**, even under `unsupportedParams: ignore` — "succeed at the
  wrong thing" is not a mode.

`tools` is handled separately from both groups. The default `server.tools: mcp`
serves caller functions through a per-conversation MCP bench and can return
`tool_calls`; `server.tools: off` drops and reports them as ignored. `tool_choice`
remains ignored because ACP cannot force the agent's selection. The classifier /
serving boundary is recorded in
[src/params.js.hint#tools_ignored](src/params.js.hint).

Unknown fields count as style: OpenAI adds parameters faster than any bridge tracks
them, and failing on an unrecognised name would age badly.

Ignored is never silent — logged once per (model, parameter), never per request, and
echoed as `x_acp2api.ignored` on the response.

`max_tokens` and `stop` are honoured **for real**, by watching the output and
cancelling the turn. They are the only two of that family that are decidable from
the output alone. Token counts are ~4 chars/token: there is no tokenizer and cannot
be a correct one, since the agent picks the model.

# bad Do not accumulate after the limit fires {#cut_drain}

Cancelling an ACP turn is not instant — the agent keeps sending for a moment. The
first version appended those chunks before re-checking `cut`, so a truncated answer
quietly grew back to full length. Once cut, skip every update until the stop message.

# rule MCP carries two distinct tool surfaces {#mcp_tools}

An agent's configured `mcpServers` are fixed, agent-owned capabilities declared on
every session creation path. The agent uses them inside its own loop and never
returns those calls for the OpenAI caller to execute.

OpenAI request `tools` are caller-owned and per conversation. With
`server.tools: mcp`, acp2api becomes their MCP server, adds that server when the
session opens, and parks the ACP turn while returning `tool_calls` to the caller;
the next request's tool results resume the same turn. These surfaces both use
`mcpServers` on the ACP wire but must never be described as interchangeable. See
[src/mcp.js.hint](src/mcp.js.hint) and
[src/params.js.hint#tools_ignored](src/params.js.hint).

ACP wants `env` and `headers` as `[{name, value}]` arrays; the config accepts a
mapping and converts. The stdio variant has **no `type` discriminator** and requires
`args` and `env` present even when empty.

# rule ACP specifies the model, and it took a live probe to know that {#acp_config_options}

The protocol docs do not describe this and a page-level read says it is unsupported.
It is supported, via `session/set_config_option`. `session/new` returns
`configOptions[]`, each with `id`, `name`, `type` (`select` | `boolean`) and a
semantic `category`:

| category | what it selects | claude's id | codex's id |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `model` | the model | `model` | `model` |
| `thought_level` | reasoning depth | `effort` | `reasoning_effort` |
| `model_config` | model parameters | `fast` | `fast-mode` |
| `mode` | permission mode | `mode` | `mode` |

**Select by category, never by id** — the table above is exactly why. `config.model`
and `config.reasoning` resolve through `category`; `config.options` is the escape
hatch that addresses a raw id.

Verified on 2026-08-09 against `claude-agent-acp` 0.66.0 and `codex-acp` 1.1.14 by
running `initialize` + `session/new` and printing the response. Do that again rather
than trusting this table: the model lists move every few weeks. The sanctioned
re-probe is `acp2api --config <file> --probe <agent-name>`; it uses the production
ACP setup path and sends no prompt.

The wider survey in [#agent_survey](#agent_survey) shows how far this table travels:
the category names are consistent across vendors, but WHICH categories an agent
publishes is not, and one of them appears only after a model has been chosen.

# rule What the registry's 38 agents actually do {#agent_survey}

Run on 2026-08-14 against the
[ACP registry](https://cdn.agentclientprotocol.com/registry/v1/latest/registry.json),
which carries the exact package or signed archive and the exact argv per agent — so
"does it work with this bridge" is answered by a loop rather than by 38 guesses.
Each was installed and asked `initialize` + `session/new`; the ones that would take
a model without an account were then driven through a real turn. The results are in
docs/agents.md. What belongs here is what they imply for this code.

**`_meta.steering.supported` is advertised by three of thirty-eight** — Claude
Agent, Codex and JetBrains Junie. `Agent#inject` already returns false without it,
which is right, but it means `server.busy: queue` is a two-vendor feature and should
be described as one rather than as a general capability.

**`mcpCapabilities` is advertised and this bridge ignores it.** Agents publish
`{http, sse}` at `initialize`; several publish neither, and Cline publishes no
`mcpCapabilities` object at all. `#mcpFor` hands every configured server to
`session/new` regardless of transport. Nothing has broken because of it yet, but an
http server given to a stdio-only agent is a failure that could be caught at config
time and is currently discovered at session time.

**Authentication failures are unstructured agent errors, but not server faults.**
An agent that refuses `session/new` until its account is signed in is in a permanent
state, not a transient one. The bridge recognizes the ACP `-32000` authentication
shape and maps it to 401 so a router does not keep retrying a broken login.

**Two shapes of "not configured" look alike and are not.** `Failed to resolve
provider: GOOSE_PROVIDER` is one config file away from working; `Authentication
required: call authenticate` is an account nobody in this repository can create.
Both arrive as `session/new` errors. Anything that classifies agents automatically
has to separate them, and the only signal available is the message text.

# bad Two failure modes that look like success {#silent_success}

Both were found by running the thing, not by reading the code, and both would make a
router treat a failure as an answer.

- **A cancelled turn is an ordinary ACP stop reason**, not an error. Returned
  unexamined, a timed-out turn is `200` carrying whatever partial text existed. The
  server tracks timeout and client-disconnect separately and answers `504` for the
  first, nothing for the second.
- **Streaming headers must not be sent before the agent starts.** Committing to
  `200 text/event-stream` up front turns a clean `429` into a stream that merely
  stops early, and the caller cannot tell the difference. Headers go out on the first
  streamed event.

Also: never fall back to a different model when the requested one is unavailable.
The caller picked that agent name to get that model; `400` is the honest answer.

# rule 429 is the contract; ACP has no quota code {#limit_detection}

An exhausted subscription arrives as a plain error string — there is no code, no
field, nothing structured. `server.limitPatterns` (regexes, config not code) is the
entire detection mechanism, and an unmatched message stays **502 on purpose**: a
router must not spend its next provider on a crash it should have retried.

Statuses are the whole interface to a failover chain — 429 try the next brain, 502
fault, 503 could not spawn, 504 no answer in time, 401 not logged in.

When a new agent phrases exhaustion differently, add a pattern; do not widen the
default set to catch it by accident.

# rule The option set changes when the model changes {#option_reapply}

Selecting a model can add or remove other options. Claude drops the `effort`
selector entirely once Haiku is chosen. So options are applied **in order** — model
first, then reasoning, then raw ids — and the list is re-read from each
`session/set_config_option` response rather than from the `session/new` snapshot.

Resolving all ids up front looks equivalent and is not: it fails with
`unknown config option "effort"` on a config that is merely contradictory.

# risk claude-agent-acp option changes contaminate the next prompt {#option_transcript}

Verified live on 2026-08-29 against `claude-agent-acp` 0.66.0: sending
`session/set_config_option` for `model=sonnet` made the next user turn see a
`<local-command-caveat>` and the complete `/model sonnet` command/result block in
its conversation. A control session with no set saw no local-command entry.

The follow-up census used the same quote-what-you-see prompt:

| channel | value | transcript verdict |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `session/set_config_option` category `thought_level` | `effort=low` | clean |
| `session/set_config_option` category `mode` | `mode=plan` | clean |
| `session/set_mode` | `modeId=plan` | clean |

Use `session/set_mode` for Claude mode because it is the dedicated clean channel.
Effort stays on `session/set_config_option`; the live census found no contamination,
and `MAX_THINKING_TOKENS=0` and `1024` both left the ACP effort `currentValue` at
`high`, so it is not an effort-category substitute.

For Claude, config normalization derives `ANTHROPIC_MODEL` from `model` before the
child starts. The values `sonnet`, `haiku`, `opus[1m]`, `claude-fable-5[1m]` and
`default` all round-trip verbatim into the model option's `currentValue` on adapter
0.66.0. Explicit `env.ANTHROPIC_MODEL` wins over the derived value. An exact live
match skips the contaminating RPC; a different offered value falls back to the RPC
with a warning, because the requested model outranks transcript cleanliness; an
unoffered value remains the existing 400 listing offered values.

Skip a set only when the option's CURRENT live `currentValue` exactly equals the
wanted value. A set that is still necessary must be sent, and its returned option
list must still replace the prior list per [#option_reapply](#option_reapply).
`--probe` exposes `currentValue` so operators can choose adapter defaults where
acceptable and avoid injecting command transcripts at all.

The operator decided on 2026-08-29 not to file the upstream
`claude-agent-acp` issue. The bridge owns this bypass; do not file the saved draft
from the plan-12 report unless the operator explicitly reverses that decision.

# good One process per agent, one live session per conversation {#lifecycle}

Spawning `claude-agent-acp` costs seconds, so the child process is lazily started
and kept.

The session was originally per REQUEST, because an OpenAI chat completion is
stateless and carries its whole history in `messages` — reusing one would replay
that history on top of the agent's own. `continuity` (1.2.0) and the caller-supplied
key (1.4.0) changed that: a session now spans a conversation. It stays resident
until its TTL or the resident cap parks it, then its id is retained and
`session/resume` revives it without replay. See
[src/sessions.js.hint#parking](src/sessions.js.hint).

`close()` is terminal. Without that, a request arriving during shutdown respawns a
CLI that nothing is left to reap, and the process never exits.

# rule The protocol surface this bridge uses and leaves unused {#unused_surface}

Taken on 2026-08-11 by loading the SDK and both adapters and printing what they
advertise. Recorded because reading the docs understates it and reading this
source overstates how much of ACP is in play.

**Session lifecycle.** Both adapters advertise `loadSession: true` and
`sessionCapabilities: { resume, list, close, delete, fork, additionalDirectories }`
(`codex-acp` minus `fork`). `session/load` replays the whole transcript back as
`session/update` notifications; `session/resume` restores the session and its MCP
connections and replays nothing — **`resume` is the one a rendering bridge wants**,
or every reconnect re-emits the history as fresh output. The bridge now uses
`resume` for parked conversations, `fork` for supported warm bases, and `close` for
cleanup. It still leaves `load`, `list`, `delete`, and additional directories
unused.

`claude-agent-acp` fingerprints `cwd` + `mcpServers` and compares on load/resume,
so a stored id is only valid while an agent's config is unchanged. Any persistence
scheme must key on that or fail closed.

**The SDK cannot help, which is why this bridge routes updates itself.** Checked
against 1.3.0, the newest published: `ActiveSession` has a private constructor,
`attachSession` is private, and `AgentContext` exposes only `request` and `notify`.
There is no public path from a session id to an `ActiveSession` — so a session
RESUMED by id could never receive its updates through one, and `session/resume`
and `session/fork` would both be unreachable.

Hence `Agent#sinks`: one `session/update` handler on the client app, fanning out by
`sessionId`, and `turn()` awaiting the `session/prompt` response directly. See
[src/agent.js.hint#own_routing](src/agent.js.hint). Do not "simplify" this back to
`builder.start()`; it would take resume with it.

**`session/update` has thirteen kinds; this bridge reads seven names.** Read:
`agent_message_chunk`, `agent_thought_chunk`, `tool_call` / `tool_call_update` and
`plan` / `plan_update` — the last four rendered as progress notes by
[src/progress.js.hint](src/progress.js.hint) when `server.progress` is on, and
still never allowed into the answer — plus `usage_update`, which feeds per-turn
usage, cost, context-fill metrics, and session retirement.

Unread: `plan_removed`, `session_info_update` (the title the agent writes itself),
`available_commands_update`, `current_mode_update`, `config_option_update`, and
`user_message_chunk` (echo of what the agent actually heard, behind
`--replay-user-messages`).

**Incremental plans are client-gated.** `plan_update` and `plan_removed` are sent
only to a client that advertises `plan: {}` in its capabilities. Omit it and the
agent either resends whole plans or sends none, which reads as an agent that does
not plan.

**A tool call is already structured.** `kind` ∈ `read | edit | delete | move |
search | execute | think | fetch | switch_mode | other`, `status` ∈ `pending |
in_progress | completed | failed`, and `content[]` entries are `content`,
`diff { path, oldText, newText }` or `terminal { terminalId }`. Anything rendering
progress should read those rather than parse tool names.

**`terminal/*` is opt-in.** With `server.terminal: true` the bridge advertises it
and owns command execution: live output, per-command kill, cwd containment,
timeouts, output bounds, and process-group reaping. It defaults off because this
transfers sandboxing and cleanup responsibility from the agent to the bridge. See
[src/terminal.js.hint#ownership](src/terminal.js.hint).

`elicitation/create` (a typed agent-initiated question, the one pause that can
carry free text) and `_meta` on nearly every message remain unused.
`session/set_mode` is used for Claude's configured mode because the dedicated
method was live-proven transcript-clean; other agents retain their advertised
config-option path. The SDK also ships `http-stream`, `ws-stream`, `server-sse` and
`ws-server`, so ACP is not stdio-only — but `claude-agent-acp` exposes no HTTP
flag, so a networked agent needs a wrapper this project does not have.

# rule No human answers a permission prompt

`session/request_permission` must be answered from config (`server.permission`),
matched on the option's `kind` and not its id. The blast radius is contained by
`server.cwd`: the `fs/read_text_file` and `fs/write_text_file` handlers refuse any
path outside it.

# good Tests drive a real ACP agent, not a mock {#fixture}

`test/fixtures/fake-agent.js` is a genuine ACP agent over genuine stdio, built on
the same SDK. Mocking the connection would only prove the code calls the functions
it was written to call; this covers the wire format, the `session/set_config_option`
round trip, streaming, cancellation and the 429/502 split — offline, and without
spending a subscription.

Its prompt text selects the failure to exercise; the binding keyword and
pre-session environment tables live in
[test/fixtures/fake-agent.js.hint](test/fixtures/fake-agent.js.hint). Add behaviour
there rather than reaching for a mocking library.

# bad Never publish a version in order to test it {#verify_before_publish}

A published version is a release, not a step in a debugging loop. On 2026-08-12
this package went 1.6.0 → 1.6.1 → 1.6.2 within an hour on a live fleet, and the
last two existed only because the previous one was wrong.

The defect that forced 1.6.2 was findable before any of them: `inject` was built
and shipped against `claude-agent-acp`, which advertises `promptQueueing`, and then
run against `codex-acp`, which does not know the word — where a second prompt into
a busy session deadlocks it and costs the caller its entire request timeout. The
raw-ACP probe that eventually found it already existed and had been used against
one adapter only.

**Verify with a packed build, against every adapter the change touches.**
`npm pack` produces the same artifact the registry would serve, and it installs
into a container exactly like a published one:

```
npm pack                                   # -> acp2api-x.y.z.tgz
docker cp acp2api-x.y.z.tgz <container>:/tmp/
docker exec <container> npm install -g --include=optional /tmp/acp2api-x.y.z.tgz
```

The test suite is necessary and not sufficient here: the fixture agent behaves the
way the adapters were *observed* to behave, so a capability no adapter was probed
for is a capability the suite cannot know about. Publish once, when it is right.

# done Acceptance

Verified 2026-08-09 on the Mac, against the real logged-in Claude subscription:

- `POST /v1/chat/completions` with `model: claude-haiku` returned `"Haiku"` with the
  thinking separated into `reasoning_content` and usage populated.
- The same request with `stream: true` on `claude-sonnet-high` produced well-formed
  SSE deltas ending in `finish_reason: "stop"` and `[DONE]`.
- The offline suite passes against the fixture.

The live run is what caught [#option_reapply](#option_reapply); the suite did not,
because the fixture had a stable option set until that finding was folded back in.
Re-run a live check after protocol-facing changes — the fixture agrees with whatever
it was taught.
